{"t": 2671, "dir": "out", "type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "id": "msg_011ChGefbsiqnKFNSNNagpEe", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# The History of Lighthouses\n\nFor as long as people have travelled by sea, they have needed ways to find their way home. The lighthouse is one of humanity's oldest answers to that problem: a fixed beacon, built on a headland, reef, or harbour, that tells a sailor where land lies and where danger waits.\n\nThe most famous early example was the Pharos of Alexandria, built around 280 BCE during the reign of Ptolemy II on the small island of Pharos. Its tower rose several hundred feet, and at night a fire burned at its top, its light reflected by polished metal. Ancient writers praised it as one of the wonders of the world. Its design influenced the word itself: in many languages, \"pharos\" or a variant of it still means l"}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 4211, "cache_read_input_tokens": 14941, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 4211}, "output_tokens": 1, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "3ec58f9d-bcc3-483a-bef3-cc0c5cbd650c", "uuid": "8a5e66e1-cbce-41bf-96fb-88c144f78771", "timestamp": "2026-11-21T19:47:13.244Z", "request_id": "req_011ChGefbj3PmW3w5yxi4DkM", "aborted": true}
